  "metadata": {
    "title": "Memory's price surge threatens Apple's 'magic formula' — and the economy: WSJ reporter",
    "byline": null,
    "published_at": null,
    "site_name": "finance.yahoo.com",
    "language": null
  },
  "content": {
    "text": "Wall Street Journal technology reporter Rolfe Winkler discusses his interview with Apple (AAPL) CEO Tim Cook, who said that rising memory costs have made iPhone price increases \"unavoidable.\"\nApple desperately needs memory for consumer. And as you could see, my crown is doing quite well. We should say that the reason this is a problem for Apple is because the prices of these components, the reason Micron stock is doing that is because uh the price of memory and storage, those have quadrupled over the past year and they're they're going to keep going up.\nNow, why is that potentially a 100-year flood? The cost of chips\nalways goes down. The cost of the components inside always is going down. This is what powers all of these technology revolutions that we get each decade. But what happens if that magic formula reverses and the cost of the chips instead of\ngoing down, suddenly reverse. And that's what you've seen with memory and storage here, and that kind of just\ncompletely ruins that recipe for for continued expansion for the kinds of devices we've come to, you know, rely on as consumers. And of course I asked him cook this. I said, you know, which devices, when and how much are we talking about when it comes to price increases? And he said we're still working through those those details.\nWe talked to tech insights, uh a research firm, and they said, look, the cost of memory and storage inside an iPhone 17 Pro, last year's device was about 50 bucks.\nFor the iPhone 18 Pro, you're looking at about 200. Now, if Apple doesn't just want to pass those costs along, it also wants to make its margin, then you're looking at a cost increase of more than $200. Now, do I think they're really going to do that?\nHard to say, but even if it's $100, maybe 200, maybe you're looking at an entry level 256 gig iPhone 18 that's 12.99\ninstead of the entry level iPhone 17 that was 10.99. Last week, Microsoft talked about the Surface is now 15.99. Two years ago it was 9.99.\nThe little flash drives you pick up at Best Buy, those have doubled or more. I don't know it's last I checked. Uh the Nintendo Switch is going up, Xboxes are going to go up, PCs are going up. This is a cost that cars, I mean,\nthe memory is a key component inside automobiles. Like there are a lot of onboard computers inside cars now. So this is a component cost that is going to ripple out across\nall of the economy.",
